data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** It works just like any other unmanaged type:

```csharp
Span<nint> span = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, each `nint`/`nuint` is 8 bytes. The only caveat is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context (since the size isn't fixed at compile time), but `stackalloc` itself works fine in safe code when assigned to a `Span<T>`.
